\caption(The native trace retains the actual system before elimination and the actual final pivots, reduced RHS and moments. Both local pieces depend on the shared interior moment.)
\alttext(Three knot boxes carry moments zero, minus three and zero. Arrows connect each adjacent pair to its actual local coefficient box; the displayed one-row system is four times the interior moment equals minus twelve.)

> Why the solution exists, and what natural means

After fixing the endpoint moments, the matrix is symmetric. Each diagonal
2(h_(i−1)+h_i) exceeds the sum of its nonnegative off-diagonal entries, so
it is positive definite. The moment system has a unique solution, and Thomas
elimination has positive pivots in exact arithmetic. Assembly, factorization,
back substitution and coefficient construction take O(n) work and storage.
The traced and ordinary fit use one executing kernel, with owned array copies
only when a trace is requested.

Natural specifies endpoint second derivatives, not endpoint slopes. It also
has a useful variational meaning. Among interpolants with a square-integrable second derivative on the knot
span [x_0,x_(n−1)], the natural spline minimizes the bending functional
E(g)=integral from the first to the last knot of (g''(x))² dx. To see why, write g=S+v, with v zero at every knot.
Integrating integral S''v'' by parts on every interval gives zero: S''' is
constant inside each piece, v vanishes at knots, S'' is continuous across
interior knots, and its endpoint values are zero. Therefore

\[
E(S+v)=E(S)+E(v)\ge E(S).
\]
\alttext(The cross term in the squared-curvature energy vanishes, leaving the spline energy plus the nonnegative energy of the perturbation.)

Equality requires v''=0, so v is a line; with at least two distinct knots it
vanishes identically. This explains the natural boundary choice and uniqueness.
It does not impose monotonicity or keep every piece inside its data range.
Floating calculations approximate this ideal system; finite checks do not
certify conditioning or an exact energy inequality.

> Retain the original Runge comparison, without clipping the polynomial

Use f(x)=1/(1+25x²) at eleven equally spaced knots from −1 to 1. These are
the original coordinates and values. The global interpolating polynomial has
degree at most ten; the natural spline has ten cubic pieces. Both interpolate
the same knot data, yet their behavior between knots can differ substantially.

The scan now uses 2,001 integer-indexed coordinates, including both endpoints,
instead of repeated floating additions of 0.001. It reports a maximum over
that finite grid, not a certified continuous supremum. The old plot's upper
limit 1.3 cut off the polynomial's largest visible overshoot; the full range
is retained below.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cubic_spline.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto f=[](double x){return 1/(1+25*x*x);};
  const dm::Vector<> xs=dm::seql(-1,1,11);
  const dm::Vector<> ys=1/(1+25*xs*xs);
  const dm::algorithms::PreparedCubicSpline p(dm::algorithms::natural_cubic_spline(xs,ys));
  const dm::Vector<> grid=dm::seql(-1,1,2001);
  const auto nodes=dm::algorithms::interpolation_error_scan([&](double x){return p.evaluate(x);},f,xs);
  const auto spline=dm::algorithms::interpolation_error_scan([&](double x){return p.evaluate(x);},f,grid);
  const auto polynomial=dm::algorithms::interpolation_error_scan([&](double x){return dm::algorithms::lagrange_interpolate(xs,ys,x);},f,grid);
  if(spline.max_abs_error<.0219 || spline.max_abs_error>.0221 || polynomial.max_abs_error<1.91 || polynomial.max_abs_error>1.92 || nodes.max_abs_error>1e-14) return 1;
  std::print("| model | queries | sampled maximum error | first worst coordinate | sampled RMS error |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| natural spline | {} | {:.8f} | {:.6f} | {:.8f} |\n",spline.queries.size(),spline.max_abs_error,spline.queries[spline.worst_index],spline.rms_error);
  std::print("| degree-10 polynomial | {} | {:.8f} | {:.6f} | {:.8f} |\n",polynomial.queries.size(),polynomial.max_abs_error,polynomial.queries[polynomial.worst_index],polynomial.rms_error);
  std::print("\nSampled knot error {:.3e}; polynomial/spline sampled maximum-error ratio {:.6f}.\n",nodes.max_abs_error,polynomial.max_abs_error/spline.max_abs_error);
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(polynomial.queries,polynomial.predictions,"degree-10 polynomial",{220,38,38},1.6)
      .line(spline.queries,spline.references,"true Runge function",{22,163,74},2)
      .line(spline.queries,spline.predictions,"natural cubic spline",{37,99,235},2)
      .points(xs,ys,"knots",{31,41,55},5)
      .legend({"degree-10 polynomial","true Runge function","natural spline"},{{220,38,38},{22,163,74},{37,99,235}})
      .title("Original eleven knots: complete polynomial overshoot")
      .x_label("x").y_label("interpolated value").x_limits(-1.05,1.05).y_limits(-.4,2.6).size(900,580)
      .save_svg("build/cubic_spline.svg");
}
```
