// result_end
\caption(The original error magnitudes remain approximately 0.02197 for the spline and 1.91564 for the polynomial. The complete vertical range shows both outer polynomial peaks, rather than hiding them above the frame.)
\alttext(A full-range plot shows both curves through eleven black knots. The global polynomial rises near two between outer knots and falls below zero farther inward; the natural spline follows the positive Runge function much more closely in this experiment.)

This is one useful interpolation comparison, not a theorem that every cubic
spline is accurate for every knot choice. Global polynomial interpolation on
unsuitable nodes can oscillate even in exact arithmetic; evaluating the same
polynomial with a different formula does not change that interpolation problem.
Likewise, natural boundary conditions can introduce their own approximation error.

> Boundary compatibility changes the refinement rate

On a uniform mesh, the interior moment equation is
m_(i−1)+4m_i+m_(i+1)=6(y_(i−1)−2y_i+y_(i+1))/h². For a smooth function,
substituting its true second derivatives leaves an O(h²) residual. The
strictly diagonally dominant interior matrix has an inverse bounded in maximum
norm independently of the grid size. If the endpoint second derivatives also
match the natural zeros, the moment errors are O(h²). They enter the local
spline formula multiplied by h², giving O(h⁴) value error together with the
local cubic interpolation remainder, under the stated smoothness and compatible
boundary assumptions.

For f(x)=x², however, the true second derivatives at both ends are two.
Writing e_i=m_i−2 gives e_0=e_N=−2 and the homogeneous interior recurrence
e_(i−1)+4e_i+e_(i+1)=0. Its decaying root is −2+sqrt(3), so an O(1)
moment mismatch persists near each boundary. On an interval, with λ=(x−x_i)/h,
the value error is

\[
S(x)-x^2=\frac{h^2}{6}\left[e_i((1-\lambda)^3-(1-\lambda))
+e_{i+1}(\lambda^3-\lambda)\right].
\]
\alttext(The quadratic's spline error equals h squared times a fixed combination of the two neighboring moment errors. The boundary moment mismatch therefore produces second-order value error.)

Thus the maximum error near the ends is of order h², despite f being a smooth
polynomial. Natural splines reproduce lines, but need not reproduce quadratics
or arbitrary cubics whose endpoint curvature violates the chosen boundary data.
The next controlled study contrasts x² with sin(πx), whose endpoint second
derivatives vanish. Every displayed error is a native finite-grid census.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cubic_spline_orders.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const double pi=std::acos(-1.);
  const dm::Vector<> grid=dm::seql(0,1,8193);
  std::vector<double> log_intervals,log_sin_error,log_quadratic_error;
  double previous_sin=0,previous_quadratic=0;
  std::print("| intervals | sampled sin error | halving ratio | sampled x squared error | halving ratio |\n| ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(std::size_t intervals:{4,8,16,32,64,128}) {
    const dm::Vector<> xs=dm::seql(0,1,intervals+1);
    const dm::Vector<> sine=dm::sin(pi*xs),quadratic=xs*xs;
    const dm::algorithms::PreparedCubicSpline ps(dm::algorithms::natural_cubic_spline(xs,sine)),pq(dm::algorithms::natural_cubic_spline(xs,quadratic));
    const auto es=dm::algorithms::interpolation_error_scan([&](double x){return ps.evaluate(x);},[=](double x){return std::sin(pi*x);},grid);
    const auto eq=dm::algorithms::interpolation_error_scan([&](double x){return pq.evaluate(x);},[](double x){return x*x;},grid);
    const auto rs=previous_sin/es.max_abs_error,rq=previous_quadratic/eq.max_abs_error;
    if(intervals>=16 && (rs<15.5 || rs>17 || rq<3.98 || rq>4.02)) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {:.6e} | {} | {:.6e} | {} |\n",intervals,es.max_abs_error,previous_sin?std::format("{:.6f}",rs):"n/a",eq.max_abs_error,previous_quadratic?std::format("{:.6f}",rq):"n/a");
    log_intervals.push_back(std::log2(intervals));log_sin_error.push_back(std::log2(es.max_abs_error));log_quadratic_error.push_back(std::log2(eq.max_abs_error));
    previous_sin=es.max_abs_error;previous_quadratic=eq.max_abs_error;
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(log_intervals,log_sin_error,"compatible sine",{37,99,235},2)
      .points(log_intervals,log_sin_error,"",{37,99,235},4)
      .line(log_intervals,log_quadratic_error,"incompatible quadratic",{234,88,12},2)
      .points(log_intervals,log_quadratic_error,"",{234,88,12},4)
      .legend({"sin(pi x)","x squared"},{{37,99,235},{234,88,12}})
      .title("Endpoint compatibility changes the global refinement rate")
      .x_label("log2(number of intervals)").y_label("log2(sampled maximum absolute error)")
      .x_limits(1.8,7.2).y_limits(-34,-5).size(900,580)
      .save_svg("build/cubic_spline_orders.svg");
}
```
