\caption(On the explicitly transformed base-two axes, slopes approach minus four and minus two. Halving h reduces the compatible sine error by about sixteen, and the incompatible quadratic error by about four.)
\alttext(Two decreasing error curves on base-two logarithmic coordinates have distinct slopes: the compatible sine falls about four vertical units per horizontal unit and the quadratic falls about two.)

These grids illustrate the derived asymptotic behavior before rounding dominates.
They do not provide rigorous continuous maximum bounds. In particular, a small
sampled error should not be relabeled a guaranteed error tolerance.

> C2 smoothness does not preserve shape, and natural extrapolation is still cubic

Fit nondecreasing samples (0,0), (1,1), (2,1). The natural spline has
S(1.5)=1.09375, above the largest sample. At x=2 its second derivative is
zero, yet its last cubic coefficient is nonzero. Extending that piece to x=3
gives S(3)=1, while extending the endpoint tangent line would give 0.75.
Natural imposes curvature at one endpoint, not a linear function everywhere
outside it. Another nonnegative dataset, (0,0), (1,0), (2,1), gives the negative
value S(0.5)=−0.09375. Neither monotonicity nor positivity follows from C2 continuity.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cubic_spline_limits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const dm::algorithms::PreparedCubicSpline p(dm::algorithms::natural_cubic_spline({0,1,2},{0,1,1}));
  const dm::algorithms::PreparedCubicSpline q(dm::algorithms::natural_cubic_spline({0,1,2},{0,0,1}));
  if(p.evaluate(1.5)!=1.09375 || q.evaluate(.5)!=-.09375 || p.evaluate(2,2)!=0 || p.evaluate(3)!=1) return 1;
  std::print("| control | native value |\n| :--- | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| monotone data: S(1.5) | {} |\n| nonnegative data: S(0.5) | {} |\n| endpoint second derivative S''(2) | {} |\n| extended cubic S(3) | {} |\n| endpoint tangent at x=3 | {} |\n",p.evaluate(1.5),q.evaluate(.5),p.evaluate(2,2),p.evaluate(3),p.evaluate(2)+p.evaluate(2,1));
  const dm::Vector<> grid=dm::seql(0,3,601);
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(grid,p.evaluate(grid),"natural cubic",{37,99,235},2)
      .points(std::vector<double>{0,1,2},std::vector<double>{0,1,1},"data",{31,41,55},5)
      .line(std::vector<double>{2,3},std::vector<double>{p.evaluate(2),p.evaluate(2)+p.evaluate(2,1)},"endpoint tangent",{234,88,12},2)
      .abline_v(2,{107,114,128},1)
      .legend({"natural cubic","endpoint tangent","data"},{{37,99,235},{234,88,12},{31,41,55}})
      .title("Smooth interpolation can overshoot; end-piece extrapolation curves")
      .x_label("x; supplied knot domain ends at 2").y_label("value")
      .x_limits(-.05,3.05).y_limits(-.05,1.3).size(900,580)
      .save_svg("build/cubic_spline_limits.svg");
  bool malformed=false,capped=false,invalid=false;
  auto bad=p.coefficients();bad.d.pop_back();
  try { (void)dm::algorithms::cubic_spline_eval(bad,1); }
  catch(const std::invalid_argument&) { malformed=true; }
  try { (void)dm::algorithms::natural_cubic_spline_trace({0,1,2},{0,1,0},2); }
  catch(const std::length_error&) { capped=true; }
  try { (void)dm::algorithms::natural_cubic_spline_trace({0,1,1},{0,1,0},1); }
  catch(const std::invalid_argument&) { invalid=true; }
  if(!malformed || !capped || !invalid) return 1;
  std::print("\nMalformed records reject; complete knot cap rejects; invalid data validates before cap: pass.\n");
}
```
