> Native contracts and verification scope

The original CubicSpline five-vector layout, fitting/evaluation names and
interpolation.hpp include path remain available. Finite increasing knots,
matching finite ordinates and complete coefficient shapes now validate explicitly.
Empty fitting input gives a zero model, a singleton gives a constant and two
knots give a line. Those degenerate conventions are library choices, separate
from the uniqueness statement for a natural interpolant through at least two knots.

A mutable public CubicSpline can be changed by its caller. Consequently,
cubic_spline_eval() checks its full record in O(n) time before binary lookup.
PreparedCubicSpline owns and validates one snapshot, then handles repeated
scalar/derivative queries in O(log n) and q-query batches in O(q log n).
Values and derivative orders zero through three are supported; the third
derivative can jump at a knot, where the right interval is selected. The last
knot uses the last interval. Outside queries extrapolate the end cubic; their
coordinates are not clamped. A structurally valid manually supplied record
need not satisfy natural boundaries or C2 continuity.

The fit trace defaults to a complete 100,000-knot cap, checked after full input
validation and before allocation. The sampled error census defaults to a complete
1,000,000-query cap, with full finite-query/callback-presence checks before
allocation or invocation. It calls each callback once per query, retains actual
values and uses first-maximum ties and a scaled RMS. Empty error input has an
explicit false existence flag and zero placeholders. Zero optional caps disable
those caps only; allocation and container limits remain. Callback exceptions
propagate. Input arrays, fitted records and diagnostics retain no borrowed views;
the prepared coefficient accessor deliberately borrows its object's const record.

Arithmetic must remain representable in the checked floating calculation.
Unrepresentable widths, secants, system entries, coefficients, query offsets or
evaluation results raise an overflow error. Ordinary rounding and extreme knot
scales can still affect accuracy; validation is not a conditioning certificate.
There is no shape-preserving modification, automatic boundary-condition choice
or adaptive global-error guarantee hidden inside this natural spline API.

No external dataset is required for these manufactured interpolation controls.
Native tests retain the original Runge result, hand-solved moments, irregular
knot continuity and actual factor replay, 1,000 random linearity controls,
all derivative-side conventions, shape/finite/cap validation, empty/constant/line
cases, both convergence families and stable large-error RMS calculations.
A permanent optional Python Fraction oracle solves the full cubic value/C1/C2/
natural-boundary constraint matrix by exact dense elimination, independently
of the native moment/Thomas formulation. It checks 102 fits, their coefficients,
moments, extrapolated values and derivatives. The focused C++ tests and native
oracle checker also run under address and undefined-behavior sanitizers.

> References

C. de Boor, [A Practical Guide to Splines](https://link.springer.com/book/9780387953663)
(2001), provides a canonical treatment of spline interpolation. The
[SciPy CubicSpline documentation](https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.interpolate.CubicSpline.html)
distinguishes natural boundary data from its default not-a-knot choice;
[PPoly](https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.interpolate.PPoly.html)
documents local-power evaluation and endpoint-piece extrapolation. These
comparisons describe semantics, not a dependency on Python in executable reports.
